Article 1: The following acts shall constitute other acts causing damage to the exclusive right to use a registered trademark of another person as provided in Item (7) of Article 57 of the Trademark Law:
(1) prominently using, as the name of an enterprise in respect of identical or similar goods, characters identical or similar to another person's registered trademark, which is likely to cause misrecognition among the relevant public;
(2) reproducing, imitating, or translating another person's registered well-known trademark or its principal part and using it as a trademark in respect of goods that are not identical or similar, which misleads the public and is likely to damage the interests of the owner of the registered well-known trademark;
(3) registering characters identical or similar to another person's registered trademark as a domain name and conducting e-commerce in related goods through such domain name, which is likely to cause misrecognition among the relevant public.

Article 3: The trademark use licenses provided in Article 43 of the Trademark Law include the following three categories:
(1) an exclusive use license means that the trademark registrant, within the agreed period, territory, and in the agreed manner, licenses the registered trademark to only one licensee for use, and the trademark registrant may not, as agreed, use the registered trademark;
(2) a sole use license means that the trademark registrant, within the agreed period, territory, and in the agreed manner, licenses the registered trademark to only one licensee for use, and the trademark registrant may, as agreed, use the registered trademark but may not license any other person to use the registered trademark;
(3) an ordinary use license means that the trademark registrant, within the agreed period, territory, and in the agreed manner, licenses another person to use its registered trademark, and may itself use the registered trademark and license another person to use the registered trademark.

Article 10: When the People's Court determines, pursuant to the provisions of Items (1) and (2) of Article 57 of the Trademark Law, that trademarks are identical or similar, it shall do so in accordance with the following principles:
(1) taking the general attention of the relevant public as the standard;
(2) making a comparison of the trademarks as a whole and a comparison of the principal parts thereof, and such comparisons shall be made separately in a state of isolation of the compared objects;
(3) when determining whether trademarks are similar, the distinctiveness and reputation of the registered trademark for which protection is requested shall be considered.
